## Cause

A constructor is hot by name (`CID_HOT_T`): a hot node seals its boxed fields at build (`rfc_seal`), and a hot node's consumer takes it through `ctr_take`, which reads the seal. A cold node's consumer takes it raw (`term_loc` and `e.mem[sp + j]`).

The facts that decide hotness are written where a type is bound: sharing the list of `Sigma<U32, U32>` heats `Sigma`, so every `Tuple` seals its fields. But `Tuple` is also built inside `build` at the type `Pw(xs)`, a family stuck on an open index. The emitter never walks that instantiation, so `P` stays cold. The sealed `P` inside the hot `Tuple` reaches `total`, which takes it raw and reads the cell instead of the node: 1 is the cell's second word.

Each ingredient is needed: a shared list of pairs to heat `Tuple`; a family to hide `P` from the facts; a two-field record fed at runtime, since a constant folds to a static; the bind to keep the index open, since a once-called helper fuses and a constant argument reduces the family.

## Possible solution: heat what a hot build holds

Hotness stays static and the runtime is untouched. Two facts are added:

1. A hot constructor heats its field types where it is built, at that build's instantiation (`emit_ctr`). At the bound type this is what `facts_hot` already does; the build site catches the instantiations the facts never saw.
2. A family stuck on an open index heats all of its arms' types, once per family (`facts_fam`). Without this the stuck application falls into the `*` fallback and heats everything, which on a real program (bendquest) heated 67 of 74 constructors.

