When a program's interaction net grows past the GPU node arena, `bend run-cu` does not report an out-of-memory condition — it exits 0 and prints `Result: <Invalid>` with the message `Unable to interpret the HVM result as a valid Bend term. (Reached Root)`. Because the exit code is 0 and no error is raised, a caller cannot distinguish "the program is wrong" from "the run exceeded GPU memory", and a long-running job silently yields a garbage result.

The same program is correct just below the threshold. Using a divide-and-conquer matmul reduced to a single f24 checksum (net size scales with M·N·K), on an RTX 4090 (sm_89):

| M=N | K | work (M·N·K) | wall | result |
|-----|---|--------------|------|--------|
| 64  | 1024 | 4.2 M  | 0.81 s | correct |
| 128 | 3072 | 50 M   | 1.51 s | correct |
| 256 | 3072 | 201 M  | 4.01 s | **`Result: <Invalid>`** |
| 384 | 3072 | 453 M  | 13.4 s | **`Result: <Invalid>`** |

Kernel launch itself works on this machine (all sub-threshold runs return the correct checksum), so this is distinct from the "Failed to launch kernels" reports (#752, #715) — the launch succeeds and the run completes, but the result is silently invalid once the net overflows.

**Expected:** on GPU memory / node-arena exhaustion, `run-cu` reports an explicit out-of-memory error and exits non-zero (as `run-c` does for its own OOM, e.g. #378), rather than returning `Result: <Invalid>` with exit 0. Ideally the arena size is configurable via a flag so larger nets can run.

