### What happened

```text
(20 to 57 seconds of nothing, then)
bend: out of memory: run again with a bigger span, as in --gpu 8GB
```


The message is right; the wait is the bug. With `--gpu 500MB` the same program spends about 2 ms on the device. `bench/runtime/hashmap` and `tree-radix` with `--gpu 1GB` waited past 120 s; `tree-matmul` reported after 39 s.

I found this on my out-of-tree AMD (HIP/ROCm) port of the GPU lane, on an RX 7800 XT. I have no CUDA or Metal hardware, so it is untested there, but the code involved is shared device code, unchanged from commit 8008146.

**Cause.** `heap_alloc_miss`, `bend2/comp.ts:3710-3722`:

```c
if ((u64)p + pages > a32_load(a32_at(H, H_CAP))) {
  err_post(H, ERR_HEAP);
  p = 0;
}
got = HEAP_OFF + ((u64)p << PAGE_BITS);
for (u32 i = 1; i <= n; i += 1) {
  H[got + ((u64)(i - 1) << cls)] = i < n ? got + ((u64)i << cls) : 0;
}
ALC_AT(e, cls) = H[got];
```

After the first failure, every lane that misses builds and adopts a free-list chain on the same page 0. `heap_alloc` (3726) then pops `ALC_AT = e.mem[h]` from cells other lanes have already filled with terms, so the next allocation uses a term as a word index and reads and writes outside the corpus. Lanes only stop at their next `err_spun` poll, once per 4096 dispatches (3615). `H_BUMP` ran 20 times past `H_CAP` in the meantime.

Work-kernel time on the file below at 440 MB, one change at a time:

| change | time |
| - | - |
| none | 20.9 s |
| poll every 256 dispatches | 1.5 to 4.3 s |
| poll every dispatch | 0.001 s, but gameoflife's device time goes from 0.02 s to 1.4 s |
| poll unchanged, no chain on the error path | 0.003 s |

The last row allocates more after the error than any other, since every allocation misses, and still drains 7000 times faster. The shared chain is the cost, not the amount of post-error work.

**The change I run with.** The host is unaffected: `err_seen` is constant 0 there.

```diff
     u32 pages = (n << cls) >> PAGE_BITS;
-    u32 p     = a32_add(a32_at(H, H_BUMP), pages);
+    u32 p     = err_seen(H) ? a32_load(a32_at(H, H_CAP))
+      : a32_add(a32_at(H, H_BUMP), pages);
     if ((u64)p + pages > a32_load(a32_at(H, H_CAP))) {
       err_post(H, ERR_HEAP);
-      p = 0;
+      ALC_AT(e, cls)  = 0;
+      ALC_LEN(e, cls) = 0;
+      return HEAP_OFF;
     }
```

The file below now reports in 0.2 s, hashmap and tree-radix at 1 GB in 0.3 to 0.4 s. With enough span, the 11 runnable `!` tests and all 16 `bench/runtime/` programs still print the same bytes as the CPU lane.

It is a partial fix: lanes still run on aliased nodes until their next poll, and a class wider than a page still writes past page 0. I did not exercise the `ERR_RING` or `ERR_DEEP` drains.

