### What happened

Bend 2 accepts two fields with the same name. The evaluator and C retain their positional values, so `first(Tag{False{}, True{}})` is `False`. The checker consequently accepts `tag_is_false`, and the program has type `Copies<False{}>`.

The JS emitter uses field names as object keys:

```js
function $tag$() {
  return run_jump($first$, [{$: "Tag", ["x"]: false, ["x"]: true}]);
}

function $first$(t_0) {
  const a_0 = t_0["x"];
  const b_0 = t_0["x"];
  return a_0;
}
```

The second `x` overwrites the first, so JS computes `tag() == True`. It then takes the `True` arm of `forge`, where `Payload(True{})` is statically `U32` and may be duplicated, while the runtime value is actually the closure returned by `capture`. That closure contains the affine `Token`, and JS calls the duplicated closure twice. This is observable wrong-code and a subject-reduction failure across a dependent kind: a runtime function with an affine capture inhabits the position checked as `Data` and is contracted.

This does not make the checker accept the opposite theorem: changing the proof to `{tag() == True{} : Bool}` is rejected with `expected False{}, observed True{}`. The accepted theorem is valid in the source theory; the generated JS violates it.

This is a Bend 2 regression of #600, which reported repeated field names in Bend 0.x and was fixed in the old Rust frontend by #614. The validation is missing again in the new frontend, and the dependent grade makes the consequence stronger than the original wrong numeric result.

It touches the same JS field-key representation as #868, but is a different failure. In #868 the emitter wrote one dotted field as `x["a.b"]` and read it incorrectly as `x.a.b`; its bracket-access fix now reads that single key correctly. Here two distinct source positions are both written and read as `x["x"]`, so the JS object representation has already collapsed them into one property.

The file uses no `@unsafe`, FFI, native law, open law, Base datatype shadowing, or floating point operation.

