## Summary

On macOS, a computation that parks on a FIFO never returns at end of file.
Both Bend lanes wait with `poll()`, and `poll()` does not report the close of
a FIFO on macOS. The `select()` call does report it. A program that reads a
FIFO to the end therefore hangs in both lanes.

A pipe, a regular file and a terminal all work. The problem is the FIFO only,
and macOS only.

## What happens

I added a `Stdin.read_line` effect through the effect path in the guide. The
effect parks on fd 0 and reads one byte per wake. It reads a pipe and a file
to the end. It hangs on a FIFO.

```sh
printf 'hello\n' | ./reader      # LINE[hello] LINE[] — done
./reader < /tmp/two.txt          # LINE[a] LINE[b] LINE[] — done
mkfifo /tmp/f; ( sleep 1; echo hi ) > /tmp/f &
./reader < /tmp/f                # LINE[hi], then nothing, forever
```

The last command never ends. The wait on the fd reports no event, so the read
never runs. Only the read can see the end of file.

The parked call is one line. This is the shape of the effect:

```c
if (w->code == EAGAIN)
  return io_wait_on(w, 0, POLLIN, 0, stdin_read_line_more);   // parks on fd 0
```

## The platform difference

One Python probe waits on a FIFO, with `poll` or with `select`. The same probe
ran on three machines:

| platform | `poll` | `select` |
|---|---|---|
| Linux 5.15 | `revents=0x10` | True |
| macOS 27.0 (build 26A428), arm64 | never returns more than 0 | True |
| iSH | never | never |

iSH emulates system calls. Do not read its column as Linux behaviour.

A pty is not affected. On macOS the same probe on a pty reports `revents=0x11`
(`POLLIN | POLLHUP`). So the failure is specific to FIFOs.

The name is what matters, not the system call that made the object.
`fstat` reports `S_ISFIFO` for a named FIFO and for an anonymous pipe. Process
substitution gives an anonymous pipe, and it works:

```sh
zsh  -c 'python3 waiter.py < <(sleep 1; echo hi)'   # poll -> [(0, 17)], read -> b''  (EOF)
bash -c 'python3 waiter.py < <(sleep 1; echo hi)'   # the same
```

Only a FIFO with a name in the filesystem fails.

## Where the runtime waits

Both lanes use `poll()`:

- The native lane calls `poll` in `io_wait`, in the C that `bend prog.bend -o
  prog.c` emits: `while (poll(fds, n, ms) < 0)`.
- The JS lane calls `io_sys().poll` in `io_wait`, in the JS that `bend
  prog.bend -o prog.js` emits.

