### What happened

The default lane and emitted JS exit successfully but print `11 ` followed by a NUL character. The native C binary prints the expected `11 22`.

`eff_name("A.run")` is `a_run`, so its two JS table keys are `a_run` and `a_run_need`. `eff_name("A.run_need")` is itself `a_run_need`. `js_lib` adds only each effect's base name to the duplicate-check array `ms`, while also emitting `n + "_need"` into `$0eff`; it therefore misses this collision.

Each foreign source is correctly isolated in its own closure after #800, but the returned objects are spread into one `$0eff` object. In the shown reachability order, the `a.js` closure is spread last, so its scheduling helper overwrites `b.js`'s effect function. `A.run_need()` then returns `{ time: true }` where the checked Bend program expects a `U32`, and `U32.show` renders the corrupted value as NUL.

Reversing the two effect calls reverses the closure order: the program happens to print `11 22`, but `b.js`'s `a_run_need` then overwrites `A.run`'s scheduling helper, so `A.run` runs immediately instead of honoring `{ time: true }`. Reachability order decides which wrong behavior occurs.

This is adjacent to #799 and #800 but is not rejected by their fixes. It reproduces on 2.0.23 and 2.0.24.

