`F32.fma(a, b, c)` computes a * b + c with one rounding. The review of #929 called FMA "the strongest starting point" for small compiler additions. Two commits on `main` at 574b6d39 (2.0.29): the addition, and the changes from this PR's review.

Tested on x86-64 (i5-12500H). The checker, the JS lane and the gate ran under bun 1.4.2 on Windows 11. The C binaries were built with clang 19 and run on Linux (WSL). I have no Mac, and under WSL the CUDA lane finds no usable device, so no GPU lane was run here. The Metal mapping is the one the review verified on a Mac.

## What changed

- **`bend2/base.bend`**: `law F32.fma`, with a, b and c all F32, after `F32.atan2`.
- **`bend2/comp.ts`**: the `f32_fma` intrinsic.
  - C calls `fmaf`. On Metal, `NATIVE.C` maps `fmaf` to `fma`, as the review asked. The mapping stays Metal-only, since C's double-precision `fma` could round twice.
  - JS calls `f32_fma`. The product of two f32 values is exact in a double, so the helper rounds the double sum with c to odd, using its TwoSum error. One `Math.fround` then gives the correctly rounded f32 (53 >= 24 + 2 bits). It keeps two scratch arrays at module level (see Measurements).
- **`tests/run/float_fma.bend`** checks four cases:
  - the low bits of x * x kept after a cancellation;
  - a sum just under a halfway point, which rounds down where mul then add ties up;
  - signed zeros;
  - nan for inf * 0.

  As the review asked, it runs the cancellation once more under `!`, so a GPU build that lacks `fmaf` fails.

No other file changes; `bend.ts` is untouched.

## Verification

All on 574b6d39.

- **Tests:** `float_fma` prints its `#|` line on three lanes: `bend file.bend`, the JS build under bun, and the C build with 1 and 4 threads. Its `!` call ran on the CPU (`--gpu on` reports no device). On the same lanes, `float_ops`, `float_roundtrip`, `num_kit`, `gpu_trig` (on the CPU) and `array_atomic_fadd` still print theirs.
- **The JS helper against glibc `fmaf`:**
  - The helper, read from `comp.ts`, matches bit for bit on 6,000,000 triples.
  - NaNs are compared as NaN only, since the JS lane does not keep their payloads (WONTFIX, #797).
  - `Math.fround(a * b + c)` differs on 124,708 of the triples, which is why the helper is not that one line.
- **`gates/repo.ts`:** 46/46. `git diff --check` passes.

## Measurements

The alternative to a primitive is emulating FMA in Bend, so I compared against `fma_emu`. It is pure Bend and uses only F32 add, sub and mul: a Dekker exact product, three TwoSums and a halfway fix. It skips special values, so a complete emulation would cost more. It matches `F32.fma` on 20M generated triples. Its source and the benchmark loops are in the reproduction below; they are scratch files, not part of the PR.

Method:
- 574b6d39, i5-12500H, 1 thread. C ran on Linux (WSL); JS ran under bun on Windows 11.
- Each program is one loop, run 21 times interleaved. The figures are the median whole-program time.
- "baseline" is the same program with the accumulator set to `x` instead of updated. It measures everything except the operation under test: startup, the loop counter, the update of `x`, and printing.
- On this machine bun itself takes about 720 ms to start (an empty script), and every JS figure includes that.

