I ran `BEND_NO_TELEMETRY=1 bun bend2/main.ts repro.bend --check-only` and sampled the child RSS with `ps -o rss` every 25 ms.

## What happened

Every file succeeds with `ALL PROOFS CHECK`, but source size grows linearly while the number of cached template instances doubles at every level.

| depth | source | instances | main time | main peak RSS |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 14 | 1,049 B | 32,767 | 3.519 s | 371,072 KiB |
| 15 | 1,116 B | 65,535 | 11.918 s | 674,928 KiB |
| 16 | 1,183 B | 131,071 | 35.279 s | 1,170,496 KiB |

Released v2.0.34 also reproduces depth 15: 10.372 s and 667,952 KiB RSS.

The existing safeguards do not stop this family. Each specialization key is short, and every chain is only 16 levels deep, below the 64-level limit. There is no bound on the total number of instances or on one template's cache width.

There is also a separate quadratic cost inside each per-template cache. On every miss, `def_inst` chooses the generated name with `Object.keys(is).length`, enumerating all earlier keys again. I built a control with the same 65,535 instances and the same maximum `Path` key depth, but split the final 32,768 leaf instances evenly between two template names. Under the same direct `book_load` plus `book_valid` harness, the concentrated file takes 11.697 s and peaks at 563,136 KiB; the split control takes 6.349 s and peaks at 457,696 KiB. The checked work and key depth are the same; only the largest cache changes from 32,768 to 16,384 entries.

## Why this is separate from the generic exponential-checker WONTFIX

This file contains no beta reduction, conversion proof, recursive definition, large normal form or invalid/dead term. It exercises the template-instantiation subsystem's explicit resource guards. The 64-level guard bounds only depth, and the ordinal lookup adds quadratic work in the number of entries already admitted by the cache.

A total/per-template instance budget would make the existing protection cover breadth as well as depth. Tracking the next ordinal in O(1), instead of rebuilding `Object.keys(is)` at every insertion, would remove the extra quadratic time and transient allocation for accepted workloads below that budget.

