### Summary

The HTTP fetch demo closes the connection after two reads, even when part of the response remains unread. A response delivered in three chunks is truncated. The reproduction below runs the compiled demo with controlled receive results and shows the body ABC being printed as AB.

### What you did

Read `demos/io_http_fetch/main.bend`. Its header comment states the design: "The response is read in two recvs, the second the tail or the EOF of Connection: close" ([main.bend:3-4](https://github.com/bendlang/bend/blob/2bfc83fd6cc64cc08c6eaa225aacb69c86072788/demos/io_http_fetch/main.bend#L3-L4)). The commit that added the demo says the same: "two sequential recvs since Connection: close bounds the body" ([c04402dc](https://github.com/bendlang/bend/commit/c04402dc1ff14f88597681ee813179b5c273007b)). `fetch_sent` calls `TCP.recv` once, `fetch_head` calls it once more, and `fetch_tail` closes the socket.

### What happened

`Connection: close` bounds the body: the server's close marks where the response ends. It does not bound the number of receives needed to reach that close. Each `TCP.recv` performs one `recv()` call and returns whatever it reads ([tcp_recv.c:10](https://github.com/bendlang/bend/blob/2bfc83fd6cc64cc08c6eaa225aacb69c86072788/bend2/effs/tcp_recv.c#L10), [tcp_recv.js:16](https://github.com/bendlang/bend/blob/2bfc83fd6cc64cc08c6eaa225aacb69c86072788/bend2/effs/tcp_recv.js#L16)), so a response that arrives in three or more pieces is cut after the second, even when it is shorter than 8192 bytes.

A deterministic run of the emitted demo, with the TCP effects replaced by a schedule that returns an HTTP header plus `A`, then `B`, then `C`, prints:

```text
close: receives=2, unread=["C"]
body="AB"
```

The complete body for that schedule is `ABC`.

### Expected behavior

Receive until EOF (an empty read), with an explicit bound on the response size, then close. A test with the header separator and body split across at least three receives prints the full body.

