### The guard, and why it is namespace-scoped

`parse_patt`'s `Var` case ([bend.ts#L1712-L1716](https://github.com/bendlang/bend/blob/b2111cf43244e65f76ddc278ee695e669f720cbf/bend2/bend.ts#L1712-L1716)):

```ts
case "Var": {
  if (book_ctr(book, parse_reso(p, t.k)) !== null) {
    throw Err(book, ctx_nil(), "a braced constructor pattern (" + t.k + " is a constructor: write " + t.k + "{}, or rename the binder)", undefined, t.s);
  }
  return parse_bind(p, t);
}
```

`parse_reso` ([L1667-L1678](https://github.com/bendlang/bend/blob/b2111cf43244e65f76ddc278ee695e669f720cbf/bend2/bend.ts#L1667-L1678))
maps a bare name to `p.ns + "." + k` unless the bare key is already taken. In `LAWS.bend` that is
`LAWS.Red`. The constructor the user means is keyed `code.Red`. `book_ctr(book, "LAWS.Red")` is null, so
the arm goes to `parse_bind` as a **binder named `Red`**, flatten makes it the default, and the second arm
is dead. `safe` becomes `λc. C.cleared(c)`.

In-file the guard *does* fire, because there `parse_reso` produces the same key the `type` block
registered. So the behaviour is not "forward references are unsupported" — it is that **one of the two
spellings of the same mistake is diagnosed and the other is silent, decided by which file the datatype
is in**.

### Why this is a fidelity defect and not a dead-code one

Expect the reply that the later arms are dead code, which the checker skips by design (WONTFIX.txt;
#1165, #1202 were closed on it). The arms are dead *because the guard did not fire*. The dead-code rule
answers "why is the second arm not checked"; it does not answer "why did `case Red:` bind". The in-file
control is the evidence: the same predicate text, same arms, same deadness — and upstream's own bespoke
message. Whatever made that message worth writing applies here, where it cannot be printed.

### What it costs at a law

`bend2`, `--verdict` and the kernel all agree — on `λc. C.cleared(c)`, which is not what `LAWS.bend` says.
The kernel, inside the book it just certified, refuses the human's reading and refutes the human's law
(both oracles run on `7f6ef51c9f75d7de` and `e15042434e73aab0`, same verbatim text):

```text
ora_human_safe : ∀x0 : code.Color -> Bool =
  λ{(,): λ{.code.Red: λ{(): (code.cleared (.code.Red, ())); λ{}}; λ{.code.Green: λ{(): (.False, ()); λ{}}; λ{}}}}

ora_src_false : ∀x0 : code.Color -> {(ora_human_safe (code.paint x0)) == (.True, ()) : Bool} = LAWS.thm
  SOME PROOFS FAIL   expected: …ora_human_safe…   observed: …LAWS.safe…              exit 1

ora_law_at_green : {(ora_human_safe (code.paint (.code.Green, ()))) == (.True, ()) : Bool} = {==}
  SOME PROOFS FAIL   expected: (ora_human_safe (code.paint (.code.Green, ())))
                     observed: (.True, ())                                           exit 1

ora_emit : ∀x0 : code.Color -> {(LAWS.safe (code.paint x0)) == (.True, ()) : Bool} = LAWS.thm
  ALL PROOFS CHECK                                                                   exit 0
```

`bend LAWS.bend` alone prints `Error: 1 TODO found.`, which is what a correct unfilled `LAWS.bend` prints,
so the cheapest check a suspicious reader runs says nothing.

### Fix direction

**The in-file face** is a timing problem and the diff below fixes it: keep the immediate check, queue it,
and re-run the queue at the end of `parse_book`, when every constructor in the file is registered.

```diff
--- a/bend2/bend.ts
+++ b/bend2/bend.ts
@@ -334,7 +334,7 @@

 // Parser
 export type File  = { str: string; ns: string; al: Record<Name, Name>; };
-export type Parse = File & { book: Book; dir: string; pos: number; stk: Array<[Name, number]>; frs: number; };
+export type Parse = File & { book: Book; dir: string; pos: number; stk: Array<[Name, number]>; frs: number; later?: Array<() => void>; };
 export type Span  = { file: File; beg: number; end: number; };

@@ -1710,9 +1710,14 @@
   const book = p.book;
   switch (t.$) {
     case "Var": {
-      if (book_ctr(book, parse_reso(p, t.k)) !== null) {
-        throw Err(book, ctx_nil(), "a braced constructor pattern (" + t.k + " is a constructor: write " + t.k + "{}, or rename the binder)", undefined, t.s);
-      }
+      const reso = parse_reso(p, t.k);
+      const bare = (): void => {
+        if (book_ctr(book, reso) !== null) {
+          throw Err(book, ctx_nil(), "a braced constructor pattern (" + t.k + " is a constructor: write " + t.k + "{}, or rename the binder)", undefined, t.s);
+        }
+      };
+      bare();
+      (p.later ??= []).push(bare);
       return parse_bind(p, t);
     }
@@ -2488,6 +2493,9 @@
   while (true) {
     parse_skip(p);
     if (p.pos >= p.str.length) {
+      for (const f of p.later ?? []) {
+        f();
+      }
       return;
     }
```

Measured with it: the four forward bare shapes (two-arm match, one-arm match, nested cons pattern, let)
are refused with the guard's message; the braced forward pattern and the bare pattern above are still
refused; the lowercase, λ-match and duplicate-binder controls are unchanged; `tests/flatten/forward_reference.bend`
still gives its pinned message. Under `--check-only`, the first 12 output lines and the exit codes were
unchanged over 1,258 copied upstream fixtures from 16 test directories on 2.0.32, and over 2.0.32's 391
`tests/flatten` and `tests/parse` fixtures run on main. Nothing under `tests/` contains the guard's
message, so the files above could go in as fixtures.

**The cross-file face needs something else, and the diff above is not it** — measured on a patched scratch
copy: the in-file control is refused as intended and the witness in this issue is still `ALL PROOFS CHECK`.
The queued check re-tests the key `parse_reso` already produced (`LAWS.Red`), which no amount of later
registration will make a constructor. Two directions, both for upstream to choose:

* **Widen the guard's lookup** to the set a *reference* would consult — the bare key and each import-alias
  expansion — rather than only `parse_reso`'s single answer. Cheap, parse-time, and it makes the message
  printable wherever the user could have meant a constructor.
* **Move the pattern-versus-binder decision to where the scrutinee's datatype is known.** A `case <Name>:`
  arm on a scrutinee of type `T` could be checked against `T`'s constructor list, which removes the
  dependence on file and order entirely. Larger, and it is the shape that makes the bare and braced
  spellings agree.

A third, narrow option if the guard is to stay as it is: refuse a bare `case <Uppercase>:` arm outright
when the scrutinee's type is not local, and tell the user to write `{}` or a lowercase binder. That is a
breaking change for anyone deliberately binding an uppercase name, which the corpus may well contain.

