### Node .bend imports with query strings or fragments bypass the loader

Current main `dbb31574`, Linux x86-64, Node 22.23.1. With the documented source loader registered (`--experimental-transform-types --import ./bend2/main.ts` for this Node version), the same real file loads normally but fails when its module URL carries a cache-busting query or a fragment.

```bend
import Base

def add(a: U32, b: U32) -> U32:
  U32.add(a, b)
```

```js
const file = new URL('./lib.bend', import.meta.url);
const B = (await import(file.href)).default;
console.log(B.add(19, 23)); // 42
await import(file.href + '?version=2');
// ERR_UNKNOWN_FILE_EXTENSION: Unknown file extension ".bend"
await import(file.href + '#module');
// ERR_UNKNOWN_FILE_EXTENSION: Unknown file extension ".bend"
```

Both failures were observed in the same process after the plain import returned 42. This blocks ordinary ESM cache-busting imports; queries and fragments are part of the module identity, not the filename extension.

`load` in `bend2/main.ts` tests `u.endsWith('.bend')` on the complete URL. It should classify the decoded local file path and preserve Node's original URL identity. Non-file and non-Bend modules must continue to the next loader. No parser/compiler API, hub resolution, gate or token-cap change is needed.

