A C effect can name a valid filled def through FID(name), as documented in guide/EFFECTS.md, but e5527d5b does not treat that reference as a liveness root. A def with no Bend callsite gets no FID declaration; clang rejects the generated C with undeclared FID_hidden. Merely retaining the declaration is not enough: its entry segment must survive reachability too.

The patch discovers FID references before lowering, retains their definitions and entry segments, and reuses the same cached foreign source for emission. Constructor CID presence tests keep their existing behavior.

Real behavior proof: build tests/io/foreign_only_fid.bend with bun bend2/main.ts tests/io/foreign_only_fid.bend -o foreign-only-fid, then run ./foreign-only-fid --threads 1 --gpu off. Main fails at clang; the patched native program prints 42. The effect actually calls hidden(41) through task_node/corpus_eval, not just a metadata lookup.

Patch: https://github.com/oxura/bend/commit/7e2e021ef6520300c610cf26489731cb32bfcc23

<details><summary>Standalone reproducer</summary>

foreign_only_fid.bend:

```bend
# A C effect calls a function that has no Bend callsite.
import Base

def hidden(n: U32) -> U32:
  U32.inc(n)

def answer() -> IO(U32):
  import "./foreign_only_fid.c"

def main() -> IO(Unit):
  do IO<Unit>:
    value : U32 <- answer()
    IO.print(U32.show(value))

#|42
```

foreign_only_fid.c:

```c
static Term answer_run(Env e, Term* f, IoWork* w) {
  (void)f;
  (void)w;
  u64 at = task_node(e, FID(hidden), TERM_HOLE, 0, 0);
  e.mem[at] = 41;
  Term result = corpus_eval(e.mem, term_tsk(FID(hidden), at));
  if (err_seen(e.mem)) err_fail("foreign-only function failed");
  return result;
}

static void __attribute__((constructor)) answer_use(void) {
  io_eff(CID(answer), answer_run);
}
```
</details>

The effect scanner's ASCII word boundary treats CID inside legal host identifiers as the CID macro. On e5527d5b, calling $CID(20) or πCID(20) fails compilation with Error: CID(20) names no constructor or def. JavaScript private #CID and identifiers containing a joiner have the same problem.

The patch requires a complete host identifier token, including Unicode ID_Continue, dollar, private-name and join-control boundaries. Comments and literals keep the existing scanner behavior; this is separate from #1604/#1653's line-continuation fix.

Real behavior proof: tests/io/effect_identifier_boundary.bend fails before in both C and JS builds. Afterward the actual native executable and Bun script each print 42. The JS program exercises dollar, Greek, private and joiner identifiers; the C program exercises dollar and Greek identifiers. Real CID(answer) registration still works.

