### What Bend should do

```python
# Code that must pass --verdict can't import Array.get_unchecked (#1880), because it
# is @unsafe. Either of these would give such code a read without the wrap mask:
#
# 1. The C lane drops the mask itself where it can tell the reads stay in range. A
#    spin whose read indices step by a fixed amount (below: i + 4 and j + 4 a step,
#    for k steps) checks its first and last index against the block once on entry,
#    then runs an unmasked copy of its body if they fit and the masked body if not.
#    The source and what --verdict checks don't change, only the emitted C.
#
# 2. A read that takes the bound as an erased proof, so it doesn't need @unsafe
#    (the spelling is yours; Array would first have to state its size, #1934):
#
#      def Array.get_in(-T: Data, a: Array<T>, +i: U32, -h: {U32.is_lt(i, <a's capacity>) == True{} : Bool}) -> Array<T> & T
#
# 1 is what our kernel would use. Its indices are U32 sums of four or five loop
# variables, so a bound proof for every read would be most of the file.
#
# The loop it's for: a 4 x 4 register tile of outer products, the inner loop of
# GEMM/SYRK. It computes c[r][c] += a[r] * b[c] over k steps, with a and b stored as
# consecutive 4-vectors. Its reads stay in range by construction. Full file under Why.
def op44(k: Nat, g: T8, +i: U32, +j: U32, c00: F32, c01: F32, ..., c33: F32) -> A16:
  match k:
    case 0n:
      match g:
        case T8{s, a0, a1, a2, a3, b0, b1, b2, b3}:
          A16{s, c00, c01, ..., c33}
    case 1n+p:
      match g:
        case T8{s, +a0, +a1, +a2, +a3, +b0, +b1, +b2, +b3}:
          op44(p, get8(s, (i + 4 : U32), (j + 4 : U32)), (i + 4 : U32), (j + 4 : U32),
            (c00 + a0 * b0 : F32), (c01 + a0 * b1 : F32), ..., (c33 + a3 * b3 : F32))
```

### Why

We keep a numeric kernel in Bend so that its laws check it. It computes F32 Gram matrices and Cholesky/triangular solves, built from 4 x 4 tiles like the one above. `--verdict` passes, and nothing it imports is `@unsafe`. On the CPU it runs 1.6-2.5x slower than OpenBLAS once the matrices are 100-600 columns wide. The reproducer below shows that the wrap mask accounts for the part of that gap that comes from Bend: without it, clang compiles the tile to the same 20-instruction loop it makes of the handwritten C.

`tile.bend` (below) runs the tile 400,000 times, 1,024 steps each, reading b at a new offset each round so that no round repeats another. `tile.c` is the same loop in C, with the same order of float operations. I built `tile.bend` with the flags `bend -o` passes to `$CC` (`clang -std=c11 -O3`). Then I rebuilt its emitted C with one change, `blk_at`'s `and` removed:

```c
INLINE u32 blk_at(Term a, u64 i, u32 lgs) {
  return ((u32)i) << lgs;   // was ((u32)i & (u32)((1ull << (blk_cls(a) - lgs)) - 1)) << lgs
}
```

That's the index #1880 emits for `Array.get_unchecked`. This edit applies it to every array access in the program, which is only safe because every read and write in this file stays in range. Every build prints `1244279347`.

Ryzen 5 5600X, Linux x86_64, clang 21.1.8, bend 2.0.39. One thread (`taskset -c 11`), user CPU seconds over five alternated rounds, min-max. The machine was busy (load average 6-13); user time doesn't count the time a process spends preempted.

| build | s | hot loop |
|---|---:|---|
| `bend -o` flags | 0.75-0.80 | 50 instructions: 8 `movss`, each after a `lea` and an `and`; 4 `mulps`, 4 `addps`, 7 shuffles |
| same, `-march=native` | 0.76-0.79 | |
| mask removed | 0.34-0.36 | 20 instructions: 2 `movups`, 4 `mulps`, 4 `addps`, 4 `shufps` |
| mask removed, `-march=native` | 0.33-0.34 | |
| C twin, `-O3` | 0.36-0.42 | 20 instructions: 1 `movups`, 4 `mulps`, 4 `addps`, 4 `shufps` |
| C twin, `-O3 -march=native -ffp-contract=off` | 0.31-0.32 | |

So clang already vectorizes Bend's tile: in both builds the sixteen sums sit in four vector registers. With the mask, it builds each vector from four masked scalar loads. Without it, it loads the vector directly. Turning on FMA (`fp contract(fast)` with `-march=native`) changes the bits (`1244279366`) but not the time (0.35 s). The mask is the only difference between this loop and the C.

#### The read one step ahead

The usual way to write these loops passes the next step's read as an argument: `op44(p, get8(s, i + 4, j + 4), ...)` above, and the dot loop in #1781. The last step therefore reads one step past the range, and the mask is what makes that read harmless. Our kernel relies on this. One loop walks a column upward with `Array.get(F32, g, (idx - dt : U32))` ahead of its match, so its last read goes below 0 and wraps. With the mask removed from all of the kernel's C, it faults there (SIGSEGV in `blk_read`). Under option 1, a loop that wants the unmasked copy would have to leave one step's cells spare after its range, since the entry check would include the look-ahead read. Under option 2, the proof would have to cover that read too. In `tile.bend` the look-ahead stays inside the array: a ends at 4,096 of 16,384 cells, and b at most 12,288.

Related: #1781 (its comments list range analysis / loop versioning as the option for code that can't use `@unsafe`), #1880 (the `@unsafe` read), #1934 (Array laws, which a read that takes a proof would need).

