8 |   {==}
```

The caret points at `->`, but the arrow is not the real problem. A def that fills a law takes bare names, as in `def zero_add(n):`. The error never says so.

Soundness is not at risk, because the fill's types still come from the law. The problem is the message: people and AI agents who write a full signature, as they do for every other def, get an error about a stray character.

Each spelling fails in a different place with a different message (lines at fa1066be):

| Spelling | Where it fails | Message |
|---|---|---|
| `n: Nat` | check after the list, `bend.ts:2522-2523` | expected a name, caret after the `)` |
| `(n) -> ...` | `parse_eat(p, ":")`, `bend.ts:2541` | expected `:` |
| `+n`, `-n` | inside `parse_tele`, `bend.ts:2480` | expected `:`, observed `)` |
| `~n` | check on the first parameter, `bend.ts:2514-2515` | expected a name |

The opposite mistake already has a clear message: `'->' (a def with no return type fills a law; no law named X is in scope)`.

Related: `n: Quant`, `-n: Quant` and `+n: Quant` are accepted and print ALL PROOFS CHECK, because the check only asks whether the type is `Quant`. The annotation is ignored and `n` still has type `Nat`. This is misleading, not unsound.

The refusal itself is correct: `tests/parse/law_fill_arrow.bend` says a fill must not state a type other than the law's. Only the message should change.

I hit this while running Claude Haiku 5.5 on the four `evals/cake` proofs. Two of the four runs got this error, and one tried several unrelated fixes before it found the rule.

### A possible fix

`AGENTS.md` keeps `bend.ts` human-written, so this is a proposal and not a PR. It gives `parse_tele` one message for fills, so it can refuse at the offending character, and it removes two special-case checks.

